      "notes": [
        "**The construct form (סְמִיכוּת, *smikhut*) is Hebrew's way of saying \"X of Y\"** without שֶׁל. תַּחֲנַת הָרַכֶּבֶת = \"the train station\", כַּרְטִיס הָרַכֶּבֶת = \"the train ticket\". The first word often changes shape a little; the second stays the same.",
        "**English does this with no marker at all**: \"train station\", \"credit card\", \"language teacher\". Hebrew is the same — two nouns side by side, nothing in between. The only difference: the order is reversed (\"station train\"), and the first word's shape changes.",
        "⚠️ **The most common change: ־ָה at the end of the first word becomes ־ַת.** תַּחֲנָה → תַּחֲנַת הָרַכֶּבֶת; אֲרוּחָה → אֲרוּחַת בֹּקֶר; תְּעוּדָה → תְּעוּדַת זֶהוּת. You met this pattern in units 10 and 11 without explanation; here it is in full.",
        "⚠️ **הַ־ goes on the SECOND word, not the first.** \"The train station\" is תַּחֲנַת **הָ**רַכֶּבֶת, not הַתַּחֲנַת רַכֶּבֶת. This is the most common A1 mistake, because English instinct puts \"the\" at the front.",
        "**רַב־קַו** (*Rav-Kav*) is Israel's public transport card — one card for buses, trains and light rail across the country. Without it, many buses won't take cash at all. It's the first thing a newcomer needs to sort out.",
        "**נְסִיעָה טוֹבָה!** (\"good trip\") = \"have a good trip\". Another option: דֶּרֶךְ צְלֵחָה (\"successful road\"), a bit more formal.",
        "**הָלוֹךְ וָשׁוֹב** literally \"going and returning\" = round trip. An ancient verb form that survives as a set phrase; memorize it whole.",
        "**תָּבִיא** and **תָּבוֹא** are easy to mix up: תָּבִיא = \"bring\", תָּבוֹא = \"come\". One letter, two meanings."
      ],
